<Base title="How we test and decide">
<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><span class="eyebrow">Methodology</span><h1>How we test and decide</h1></div></section>
<div class="container section" style="padding-top:var(--space-6)"><div class="prose">
<h2>Tools</h2><p>Our tool list comes from public research. Each tool shows its price type, the tasks it helps with, and a link to its website. Tools marked “Not tested yet” have not been tried by an editor. Testing notes, pricing tables and privacy facts are added as we check each tool.</p>
<h2>Job safety tracker</h2><p>Each job gets one of three outlooks: <strong>✓ Mostly steady</strong>, <strong>◐ Changing</strong>, or <strong>↻ Big shift ahead</strong>. The outlook comes from our editorial estimate of when technology could do most of the job’s current tasks at scale:</p>
<ul><li>Range starts in 2035 or earlier: Big shift ahead</li><li>Range starts 2036–2045: Changing</li><li>Range starts after 2045: Mostly steady</li></ul>
<p>The estimates combine Frey &amp; Osborne (2013), Eloundou et al. (2023), ILO (2023) and McKinsey (2023), adjusted for physical work, licensing and BLS/WEF outlooks. None of these sources publishes per-job dates. Confidence is low for most jobs. These are estimates, not predictions or advice.</p>
<h2>News</h2><p>Headlines are collected automatically from public RSS feeds every few hours. We show the headline, a short excerpt, the source and the time, and always link to the original. Stories are sorted into topics by keywords. AI-written summaries, if we add them, will be labelled.</p>
</div></div>
</Base>
